-- ============================================================
-- FACTUAL VERIFICATION CONTROL (Passage B only; Passage A is fiction,
-- no real-world factual claims) -- COMPLETE REGISTER, all 4 externally
-- verifiable claims in the passage, not merely the 2 originally
-- disclosed (Decision 238 remediation, per the Founder's own explicit
-- "complete factual verification BEFORE independent review registration"
-- instruction -- this migration has never been registered for review
-- with an incomplete register live, since migration 162 has also never
-- been applied).
--
--   CLAIM 1: "In 1838... she travelled from Bristol... to New York in
--   about fifteen days."
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: the Great Western's maiden voyage departed
--     Bristol 8 April 1838 and reached New York in 15 (some sources:
--     15.5) days; regular service over 1838-1840 averaged about 16 days
--     westbound. Sources: Wikipedia "SS Great Western"; Britannica
--     "Great Western (ship)"; History Skills "What made the SS Great
--     Western the first Atlantic steamship".
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: "about fifteen days" used as a single,
--     clean, memorable figure rather than distinguishing the maiden
--     voyage from the regular-service average, or westbound from
--     eastbound (13 days).
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH (multiple independent, mutually-
--     consistent sources).
--     CONTESTED/QUALIFICATION STATUS: none for the figure itself.
--
--   CLAIM 2: sailing crossings "usually took about a month" and "could
--   sometimes stretch on for six weeks or more."
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: The Geography of Transport Systems' own liner-
--     crossing-times data -- contemporary sailing packet ships averaged
--     roughly 33 days westbound, 22 days eastbound, in this era.
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: rounded to "about a month" (westbound, the
--     direction this passage's own comparison uses), with "six weeks or
--     more" as the disclosed, genuinely-occurring upper bound when
--     becalmed, rather than citing the precise 33-day mean.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH.
--     CONTESTED/QUALIFICATION STATUS: none for the comparison itself.
--
--   CLAIM 3: the Great Western was "one of the first steamships built
--   specially to cross the Atlantic, rather than being adapted from a
--   ship designed for shorter journeys."
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: the Great Western was purpose-designed by Brunel
--     for the transatlantic route. Source: Wikipedia "Great Western
--     Steamship Company".
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: none needed -- the claim is already
--     carefully qualified.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH for the claim AS WORDED.
--     CONTESTED/QUALIFICATION STATUS: GENUINELY CONTESTED AND
--     DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED -- this passage never asserts the Great
--     Western was "the first" steamship to cross the Atlantic in any
--     absolute sense. The SS Sirius technically reached New York about
--     a day before the Great Western in April 1838, but Sirius was an
--     adapted coastal packet steamer pressed into an opportunistic
--     crossing, not purpose-built for the route -- exactly why this
--     passage's own wording is qualified ("one of the first... built
--     specially") rather than making any unqualified "first" claim, per
--     this project's own explicit "if a claim is contested, express the
--     uncertainty accurately, remove the claim, or choose a better-
--     supported subject" instruction: here, the qualified wording itself
--     IS the resolution, since the pedagogical content (the TIME
--     comparison) never depended on an unqualified "first" claim.
--
--   CLAIM 4: steam crossings carried "a genuine danger" of running out
--   of coal, "which early engineers took very seriously," and required
--   "constant attention from a crew of engineers, working in hot, noisy
--   conditions below deck."
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: Dionysius Lardner's 1836 argument to the British
--     Association that no ship could carry enough coal for a direct
--     Atlantic crossing was a real, documented historical episode;
--     Brunel's engineering response (a ship's coal capacity scales with
--     the cube of its length, while coal burn rate scales only with the
--     square) is the real, documented reason larger ships solved the
--     problem. Coal-fired engine-room conditions are separately and
--     extensively documented as genuinely hot (commonly 110-160
--     Fahrenheit) and loud, requiring continuous stoking. Sources:
--     World History Encyclopedia ("SS Great Britain"); Wikipedia "Great
--     Western Steamship Company"; Engines of Our Ingenuity, "Steam
--     Across the Atlantic"; shiphistory.org, "Boiler Operators and the
--     Black Gang"; The History Press, "Down amongst Titanic's Black
--     Gang" (this last source describes early-1900s conditions
--     specifically, used here only as corroboration that coal-fired
--     engine rooms were genuinely hot and loud throughout the whole
--     steam era -- the physics of a coal-fired boiler did not change
--     between 1838 and 1900 -- not as a source for any 1838-specific
--     number, and no 1838-specific number is claimed).
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: the passage states the general risk and
--     general working conditions without naming Lardner, the cube-
--     square engineering detail, or any specific temperature figure --
--     appropriately simplified for age, and deliberately not over-
--     precise about a detail this passage does not need to be precise
--     about.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH.
--     CONTESTED/QUALIFICATION STATUS: none. NOTE: this claim was NOT
--     independently source-checked when this migration was first
--     authored (Decision 237) -- the original header disclosed only
--     Claims 1 and 2. It was verified, and this register completed, only
--     during the Founder's own subsequent inspection and this
--     migration's own Decision 238 remediation. Disclosed here plainly,
--     not silently backfilled as though it had always been checked.
--
