('eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q02', 'english', 'QT-RC-04', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 150,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q02","marks":4,"skill":"vocabulary","question":"Write a synonym (a word or short phrase with the same meaning) for each of the following words as used in the passage: (a) 'reassuring' (in 'reassuring rather than frustrating') -- comforting/puts your mind at ease (this one is done for you as an example); (b) 'frustrating'; (c) 'disbelieving'; (d) 'triumphant'; (e) 'uselessly'.","modelAnswer":"(a) reassuring: comforting/puts your mind at ease (given as the worked example; not separately scored). (b) frustrating: annoying/irritating. (c) disbelieving: doubtful/not believing. (d) triumphant: victorious/celebratory. (e) uselessly: pointlessly/ineffectively.","passageTitle":"The Loose Connection","passageText":"\"Ninety minutes,\" Mr Adeyemi said, not looking up from his watch. \"Ninety minutes until the judges call your team number, and right now your robot won't move.\"\n\nHe didn't need to tell us that. I could see it for myself: Nisha crouched over the control box with a screwdriver, Ade sitting cross-legged beside the wheels doing nothing that looked, to me, like actual work, and our robot sitting exactly where it had stalled twenty minutes earlier, one wheel twitching uselessly whenever Nisha pressed the forward button.\n\n\"It's the motor,\" Nisha said, for the third time. \"Has to be. I'm going to swap it for the spare.\"\n\nI thought she was probably right. Nisha had built more of the drivetrain than either of us, and when something electrical went wrong, it was usually the thing she guessed first. I told her to go ahead.\n\nAde didn't say anything. He picked up the whole robot, tilted it gently onto its side, and started running one finger slowly along every wire between the battery and the wheels, the way you'd check a seam for a loose thread.\n\n\"We don't have time for that,\" I said. \"Nisha's already got the spare motor out.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Ade said, not stopping. \"I'm just checking first.\"\n\nI felt a flash of irritation I wasn't proud of. Ade was careful almost to a fault, the kind of person who read every instruction twice before starting, and usually I found that reassuring rather than frustrating. Today it felt like watching someone rearrange furniture while the house was on fire.\n\nNisha had the new motor half-fitted when Ade finally spoke again. \"Found it,\" he said, quietly, holding up a single wire near the battery terminal. The connector on the end had come half loose, not fully disconnected, just loose enough that it would carry power sometimes and not others, depending on how the robot was sitting.\n\nNisha stopped, the spare motor still in her hand. \"That's not the motor at all.\"\n\n\"No,\" Ade said. He didn't sound triumphant about it, which somehow made it worse. He just reconnected the wire properly, pressed it down until it clicked, and set the robot back on its wheels.\n\nNisha pressed the forward button. The robot rolled smoothly across the workshop floor and stopped exactly where it was supposed to.\n\nNobody said anything for a moment. Then Nisha laughed, short and disbelieving. \"I was about to take the whole drivetrain apart for nothing.\"\n\n\"You weren't wrong to check the motor,\" Ade said. \"It's usually the motor. This time it wasn't.\"\n\nI looked at the clock. Sixty-eight minutes left, plenty of time now, and I found myself thinking less about the competition and more about how close we'd come to spending all of it chasing the wrong problem, because the two loudest voices in the room, mine included, had been so certain they already knew the answer.\n\n\"Next time,\" I said, \"we let Ade check the wires first.\"\n\nAde almost smiled. \"Next time,\" he said, \"check the wires first anyway.\"","acceptedAnswers":["annoying","irritating","exasperating","aggravating","not believing","doubtful","incredulous","skeptical","victorious","celebratory","proud of winning","gloating","pointlessly","ineffectively","to no purpose","in vain"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 002 (Decision 237). REMEDIATION (Founder Educational Review): original Q2 asked ''how many minutes were left'', a near-identical single-fact retrieval operation to Q1 with no skill variation -- replaced, not merely relabelled, with a QT-RC-04 synonym-list question (an already-evidenced Question Type this passage naturally supports, matching the estate''s own five-item/worked-example format exactly), increasing the question family''s transfer/comprehension breadth. Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3. All 5 words independently re-checked as appearing in this migration''s own passage text.', 2, 'eng-inc002-roboticsfinal',
 'eng-inc002-qt-rc-04-roboticsfinal', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true, 'Guessing a synonym from a word''s sound or a loosely related idea rather than the meaning the word actually carries in its specific sentence context.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER'),

('eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q03', 'english', 'QT-RC-02', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 120,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q03","marks":4,"skill":"inference","question":"Does the narrator initially agree with Ade's decision to check the wires instead of letting Nisha swap the motor? Tick Yes or No, then give two reasons for your answer, using evidence from the passage.","modelAnswer":"No. Before Ade begins checking, the narrator already says 'I thought she was probably right' about Nisha's motor diagnosis and 'I told her to go ahead' with the swap -- showing early alignment with Nisha over Ade's own approach. Once Ade starts checking, the narrator explicitly says 'We don't have time for that' and admits to feeling 'a flash of irritation I wasn't proud of'. Any two of these four moments are valid, defensible evidence for the same underlying disagreement with Ade's approach.","passageTitle":"The Loose Connection","passageText":"\"Ninety minutes,\" Mr Adeyemi said, not looking up from his watch. \"Ninety minutes until the judges call your team number, and right now your robot won't move.\"\n\nHe didn't need to tell us that. I could see it for myself: Nisha crouched over the control box with a screwdriver, Ade sitting cross-legged beside the wheels doing nothing that looked, to me, like actual work, and our robot sitting exactly where it had stalled twenty minutes earlier, one wheel twitching uselessly whenever Nisha pressed the forward button.\n\n\"It's the motor,\" Nisha said, for the third time. \"Has to be. I'm going to swap it for the spare.\"\n\nI thought she was probably right. Nisha had built more of the drivetrain than either of us, and when something electrical went wrong, it was usually the thing she guessed first. I told her to go ahead.\n\nAde didn't say anything. He picked up the whole robot, tilted it gently onto its side, and started running one finger slowly along every wire between the battery and the wheels, the way you'd check a seam for a loose thread.\n\n\"We don't have time for that,\" I said. \"Nisha's already got the spare motor out.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Ade said, not stopping. \"I'm just checking first.\"\n\nI felt a flash of irritation I wasn't proud of. Ade was careful almost to a fault, the kind of person who read every instruction twice before starting, and usually I found that reassuring rather than frustrating. Today it felt like watching someone rearrange furniture while the house was on fire.\n\nNisha had the new motor half-fitted when Ade finally spoke again. \"Found it,\" he said, quietly, holding up a single wire near the battery terminal. The connector on the end had come half loose, not fully disconnected, just loose enough that it would carry power sometimes and not others, depending on how the robot was sitting.\n\nNisha stopped, the spare motor still in her hand. \"That's not the motor at all.\"\n\n\"No,\" Ade said. He didn't sound triumphant about it, which somehow made it worse. He just reconnected the wire properly, pressed it down until it clicked, and set the robot back on its wheels.\n\nNisha pressed the forward button. The robot rolled smoothly across the workshop floor and stopped exactly where it was supposed to.\n\nNobody said anything for a moment. Then Nisha laughed, short and disbelieving. \"I was about to take the whole drivetrain apart for nothing.\"\n\n\"You weren't wrong to check the motor,\" Ade said. \"It's usually the motor. This time it wasn't.\"\n\nI looked at the clock. Sixty-eight minutes left, plenty of time now, and I found myself thinking less about the competition and more about how close we'd come to spending all of it chasing the wrong problem, because the two loudest voices in the room, mine included, had been so certain they already knew the answer.\n\n\"Next time,\" I said, \"we let Ade check the wires first.\"\n\nAde almost smiled. \"Next time,\" he said, \"check the wires first anyway.\"","quotationRequired":["i thought she was probably right","i told her to go ahead","we don't have time for that","a flash of irritation i wasn't proud of"],"validationTier":"TIER3_QUOTATION_PLUS_EXPLANATION"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 002 (Decision 237). QT-RC-02, competency RC-02, passage "The Loose Connection". Confidence HIGH, EMC-4 (tick-box + N written reasons, structurally identical to migrations 097/152''s own established convention). REMEDIATION (Founder Educational Review): the accepted-evidence set was too narrow, requiring only 2 specific quotations (both about Ade directly) when the passage also supports the same judgement via 2 earlier, equally legitimate quotations showing the narrator''s prior alignment with Nisha (''I thought she was probably right'', ''I told her to go ahead''). Broadened to 4 accepted quotations; the scoring mechanism (checkQuotationPresent via .some()) already credits ANY one of the listed quotations, so this broadening genuinely increases the range of rewarded, defensible answers rather than merely restating the same 2.', 2, 'eng-inc002-roboticsfinal',
 'eng-inc002-qt-rc-02-roboticsfinal', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true, 'Assuming that because the narrator later THANKS Ade ("Next time... we let Ade check the wires first"), the narrator must have agreed with him from the start, rather than recognising the passage''s own words describe real, contemporaneous impatience.',
 'MIXED_TRANSFER'),

('eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q04', 'english', 'QT-RC-03', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q04","marks":1,"skill":"vocabulary","question":"Explain what you think the phrase 'to a fault' means, as used to describe how careful Ade was.","modelAnswer":"It means to an extreme or excessive degree -- Ade was so careful that it almost became a slight problem or annoyance, not simply a helpful quality.","passageTitle":"The Loose Connection","passageText":"\"Ninety minutes,\" Mr Adeyemi said, not looking up from his watch. \"Ninety minutes until the judges call your team number, and right now your robot won't move.\"\n\nHe didn't need to tell us that. I could see it for myself: Nisha crouched over the control box with a screwdriver, Ade sitting cross-legged beside the wheels doing nothing that looked, to me, like actual work, and our robot sitting exactly where it had stalled twenty minutes earlier, one wheel twitching uselessly whenever Nisha pressed the forward button.\n\n\"It's the motor,\" Nisha said, for the third time. \"Has to be. I'm going to swap it for the spare.\"\n\nI thought she was probably right. Nisha had built more of the drivetrain than either of us, and when something electrical went wrong, it was usually the thing she guessed first. I told her to go ahead.\n\nAde didn't say anything. He picked up the whole robot, tilted it gently onto its side, and started running one finger slowly along every wire between the battery and the wheels, the way you'd check a seam for a loose thread.\n\n\"We don't have time for that,\" I said. \"Nisha's already got the spare motor out.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Ade said, not stopping. \"I'm just checking first.\"\n\nI felt a flash of irritation I wasn't proud of. Ade was careful almost to a fault, the kind of person who read every instruction twice before starting, and usually I found that reassuring rather than frustrating. Today it felt like watching someone rearrange furniture while the house was on fire.\n\nNisha had the new motor half-fitted when Ade finally spoke again. \"Found it,\" he said, quietly, holding up a single wire near the battery terminal. The connector on the end had come half loose, not fully disconnected, just loose enough that it would carry power sometimes and not others, depending on how the robot was sitting.\n\nNisha stopped, the spare motor still in her hand. \"That's not the motor at all.\"\n\n\"No,\" Ade said. He didn't sound triumphant about it, which somehow made it worse. He just reconnected the wire properly, pressed it down until it clicked, and set the robot back on its wheels.\n\nNisha pressed the forward button. The robot rolled smoothly across the workshop floor and stopped exactly where it was supposed to.\n\nNobody said anything for a moment. Then Nisha laughed, short and disbelieving. \"I was about to take the whole drivetrain apart for nothing.\"\n\n\"You weren't wrong to check the motor,\" Ade said. \"It's usually the motor. This time it wasn't.\"\n\nI looked at the clock. Sixty-eight minutes left, plenty of time now, and I found myself thinking less about the competition and more about how close we'd come to spending all of it chasing the wrong problem, because the two loudest voices in the room, mine included, had been so certain they already knew the answer.\n\n\"Next time,\" I said, \"we let Ade check the wires first.\"\n\nAde almost smiled. \"Next time,\" he said, \"check the wires first anyway.\"","acceptedAnswers":["excessively","to an extreme degree","so much that it becomes a slight problem or downside","almost too much of a good quality","overly"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 002 (Decision 237). QT-RC-03, competency RC-03, passage "The Loose Connection". Confidence LOW, EMC-2, mirroring the Framework''s own single-instance/single-year vocabulary-explanation type -- included for portfolio-wide coverage, not treated as more established than a comparable type elsewhere in the estate.', 2, 'eng-inc002-roboticsfinal',
 'eng-inc002-qt-rc-03-roboticsfinal', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true, 'Interpreting ''to a fault'' as meaning Ade actually had a fault or flaw in his character, rather than recognising the idiom describes a GOOD quality (carefulness) taken slightly too far.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER'),

('eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q05', 'english', 'QT-RC-05', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 120,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc002-roboticsfinal-q05","marks":2,"skill":"evidence","question":"Find a quotation from the passage that shows the narrator's feelings about the competition had changed by the end. Explain what the quotation shows.","modelAnswer":"The narrator says they 'found myself thinking less about the competition and more about how close we'd come to spending all of it chasing the wrong problem', showing that by the end they were more focused on the lesson about assuming too quickly than on winning the competition itself.","passageTitle":"The Loose Connection","passageText":"\"Ninety minutes,\" Mr Adeyemi said, not looking up from his watch. \"Ninety minutes until the judges call your team number, and right now your robot won't move.\"\n\nHe didn't need to tell us that. I could see it for myself: Nisha crouched over the control box with a screwdriver, Ade sitting cross-legged beside the wheels doing nothing that looked, to me, like actual work, and our robot sitting exactly where it had stalled twenty minutes earlier, one wheel twitching uselessly whenever Nisha pressed the forward button.\n\n\"It's the motor,\" Nisha said, for the third time. \"Has to be. I'm going to swap it for the spare.\"\n\nI thought she was probably right. Nisha had built more of the drivetrain than either of us, and when something electrical went wrong, it was usually the thing she guessed first. I told her to go ahead.\n\nAde didn't say anything. He picked up the whole robot, tilted it gently onto its side, and started running one finger slowly along every wire between the battery and the wheels, the way you'd check a seam for a loose thread.\n\n\"We don't have time for that,\" I said. \"Nisha's already got the spare motor out.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Ade said, not stopping. \"I'm just checking first.\"\n\nI felt a flash of irritation I wasn't proud of. Ade was careful almost to a fault, the kind of person who read every instruction twice before starting, and usually I found that reassuring rather than frustrating. Today it felt like watching someone rearrange furniture while the house was on fire.\n\nNisha had the new motor half-fitted when Ade finally spoke again. \"Found it,\" he said, quietly, holding up a single wire near the battery terminal. The connector on the end had come half loose, not fully disconnected, just loose enough that it would carry power sometimes and not others, depending on how the robot was sitting.\n\nNisha stopped, the spare motor still in her hand. \"That's not the motor at all.\"\n\n\"No,\" Ade said. He didn't sound triumphant about it, which somehow made it worse. He just reconnected the wire properly, pressed it down until it clicked, and set the robot back on its wheels.\n\nNisha pressed the forward button. The robot rolled smoothly across the workshop floor and stopped exactly where it was supposed to.\n\nNobody said anything for a moment. Then Nisha laughed, short and disbelieving. \"I was about to take the whole drivetrain apart for nothing.\"\n\n\"You weren't wrong to check the motor,\" Ade said. \"It's usually the motor. This time it wasn't.\"\n\nI looked at the clock. Sixty-eight minutes left, plenty of time now, and I found myself thinking less about the competition and more about how close we'd come to spending all of it chasing the wrong problem, because the two loudest voices in the room, mine included, had been so certain they already knew the answer.\n\n\"Next time,\" I said, \"we let Ade check the wires first.\"\n\nAde almost smiled. \"Next time,\" he said, \"check the wires first anyway.\"","quotationRequired":["found myself thinking less about the competition and more about how close we'd come to spending all of it chasing the wrong problem"],"validationTier":"TIER3_QUOTATION_PLUS_EXPLANATION"}$json$,
