r boarding a steamship like the Great Western could expect something closer to a fixed timetable, since the engine did not care whether the wind was blowing or not. This predictability was, in many ways, just as important as the extra speed: shipping companies could begin to promise a departure date and an arrival date, and reasonably expect to keep to both.\n\nSteam crossings were not without their own difficulties, however. A steamship needed to carry an enormous quantity of coal, since every day at sea meant more coal burned, and running out partway across the ocean was a genuine danger early engineers took very seriously. Steam engines also required constant attention from a crew of engineers, working in hot, noisy conditions below deck, quite unlike the comparatively quiet work of adjusting sails above deck on a sailing ship. For some years after 1838, sailing ships and steamships shared the ocean, each suited to slightly different journeys and different kinds of cargo, before steam power gradually became the standard for carrying passengers across the Atlantic.\n\nWhat the story of the Great Western shows, above all, is that being faster was not the only advantage steam power offered. Reliability, the simple ability to know roughly when a ship would arrive, turned out to matter just as much as how quickly it could get there.","acceptedAnswers":["continuous","non-stop","ongoing","without a break","unceasing"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
